      "text": "Given the equation $7^{x+7} = 8^x$, we start by applying logarithms to both sides to bring down the exponents:\n\n1. Start with the original equation: $7^{x+7} = 8^x$.\n2. Take the logarithm on both sides: $\\log(7^{x+7}) = \\log(8^x)$.\n3. Apply the logarithm power rule: $(x+7)\\log{7} = x\\log{8}$.\n4. Distribute and solve for $x$: $x\\log{7} + 7\\log{7} = x\\log{8}$\n5. Isolate $x$: $x\\log{7} - x\\log{8} = -7\\log{7}$\n6. Factor to get $x(\\log{7} - \\log{8}) = -7\\log{7}$\n7. Solve for $x$: $x = \\frac{-7\\log{7}}{\\log{7} - \\log{8}}$\n8. Simplify the fraction using properties of logarithms:\n9. $\\frac{\\log{7} - \\log{8}}{\\log{8}} = \\log_a 7 = \\frac{-7\\log{7}}{\\log{7} - \\log{8}}$\n10. Rearrange to solve for $b$ using the given solution form: $x = \\log_b 7^7$\n11. From step 8, we have $\\log_b 7^7 = \\log_b (b^7 \\log{7})$\n12. Since $\\log_a x = b$ implies $x = b^b$, we can equate and find $b$: \n13. $7^7 = b^b$\n14. $(7^3)^7 = (7^4)^4 \\cdot 7^7 = 49^4 \\cdot 1^7$ \n15. So, we have $b^b = 49^4$ \n\nTherefore, $b = \\boxed{49}$.\n\nThis shows that the base of the logarithm in the solution form $x = \\log_b 7^7$ is $49$.",
