      "text": "To find the value of \\( b \\) in the logarithmic form \\( x = \\log_b 7^7 \\) where the solution to the equation \\( 7^{x+7} = 8^x \\), we will solve the original equation step-by-step and then express the solution in the required logarithmic form.\n\nThe equation is \\( 7^{x+7} = 8^x \\).\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the equation using a common logarithm. Taking the base-10 logarithm of both sides, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\log(7^{x+7}) = \\log(8^x)\n\\]\n\nUsing the property of logarithms \\(\\log(a^b) = b \\log(a)\\), we can simplify the equation to:\n\n\\[\n(x+7) \\log(7) = x \\log(8)\n\\]\n\nExpanding and simplifying the above equation, we have:\n\n\\[\nx \\log(7) + 7 \\log(7) = x \\log(8)\n\\]\n\nNow, we can isolate \\( x \\) by first moving all terms involving \\( x \\) to one side of the equation. Subtract \\( x \\log(8) \\) from both sides:\n\n\\[\n\\log(7) x - x \\log(8) = -7 \\log(7)\n\\]\n\nFactor out \\( x \\) on the left-hand side:\n\n\\[\nx (\\log(7) - \\log(8)) = -7 \\log(7)\n\\]\n\nNow, solve for \\( x \\) by dividing both sides by \\( \\log(7) - \\log(8) \\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-7 \\log(7)}{\\log(7) - \\log(8)}\n\\]\n\nThis gives us the solution in the form:\n\n\\[\nx = \\log_b 7^7\n\\]\n\nTo find \\( b \\), we recognize that the equation \\( x = \\log_b 7^7 \\) implies \\( b = 8 \\). To verify, we can substitute \\( x = \\frac{-7 \\log(7)}{\\log(7) - \\log(8)} \\) back into the original equation \\( 7^{x+7} = 8^x \\) and show that it holds true for \\( b = 8 \\).\n\nWe know that:\n\n\\[\n7^{x+7} = 7^{\\frac{-7 \\log(7)}{\\log(7) - \\log(8)} + 7} = 7^{\\frac{-7 \\log(7) + 7 \\log(7) - 7 \\log(8)}{\\log(7) - \\log(8)}\n}\n\n\\]\n\nAnd:\n\n\\[\n8^x = 8^{\\frac{-7 \\log(7)}{\\log(7) - \\log(8)}}\n\n\\]\n\nTherefore, from the above derivations it can be seen that \\( b = 8 \\). Therefore, the solution is:\n\n\\boxed{8}",
