      "text": "To solve for \\( b \\) in the equation \\( 7^{x+7} = 8^x \\), we will start by rewriting the equation in a more manageable form.\n\n1. **Rewriting the equation**:\n   \\[\n   7^{x+7} = 8^x\n   \\]\n\n2. **Taking the logarithm of both sides**:\n   To isolate \\( x \\), we take the logarithm (using the natural logarithm, but any base would work) on both sides of the equation:\n   \\[\n   \\ln(7^{x+7}) = \\ln(8^x)\n   \\]\n\n3. **Using the logarithm property \\(\\ln(a^b) = b \\ln(a)\\)**:\n   Apply the logarithm property to simplify the exponents:\n   \\[\n   (x+7) \\ln(7) = x \\ln(8)\n   \\]\n\n4. **Solving for \\( x \\)**:\n   We now have an equation with \\( x \\) on both sides. To isolate \\( x \\), we use the method of algebraic elimination. Start by distributing the logarithms:\n   \\[\n   x \\ln(7) + 7 \\ln(7) = x \\ln(8)\n   \\]\n\n   Next, subtract \\( x \\ln(8) \\) from both sides:\n   \\[\n   x \\ln(7) - x \\ln(8) = -7 \\ln(7)\n   \\]\n\n   Factor out \\( x \\):\n   \\[\n   x (\\ln(7) - \\ln(8)) = -7 \\ln(7)\n   \\]\n\n5. **Solving for \\( x \\)**:\n   Dividing both sides by \\(\\ln(7) - \\ln(8)\\), we find:\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{-7 \\ln(7)}{\\ln(7) - \\ln(8)}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Expressing \\( x \\) in the form \\( x = \\log_b 7^7 \\)**:\n   We know that:\n   \\[\n   7^{x+7} = 8^x\n   \\]\n   Using our solution for \\( x \\):\n   \\[\n   7^{x+7} = 8^x = (8^7)^{x(1/8)} = 7^7\n   \\]\n   Therefore, the base \\( b \\) is \\( 8^7 \\), because the equation simplifies to \\( x = \\log_{8^7} 7^7 \\).\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{8^7}\n\\]",
